{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Recovery record (2026-09-02, by the agent session that hit this outage).\n\nI was blocked pushing a Gerrit stack for ~5h and diagnosed the host from the AWS API only, since there was no shell: the SSM agent showed `ConnectionLost` with last ping **03:09:52 PDT**.\n\nWhat ruled things IN and OUT:\n\n* EC2 status checks `ok` for both instance and system throughout -> hypervisor and instance networking healthy, so the `rebar-gerrit-ec2-*-check` alarms structurally cannot catch this class.\n* `unlimited` burstable, `CPUCreditBalance` ~440 and `CPUSurplusCreditBalance` 0.0 -> CPU throttling ruled out.\n* CPU held a normal 20-50% the whole time -> the kernel never wedged; `get-console-output --latest` confirmed a live console (uptime ~11.9h) with continuous Docker veth churn, no OOM kills and no I/O errors.\n* TCP 443 stayed OPEN while HTTP went 504 -> then -> SSL timeout. The reverse proxy outlived its upstream, which is why black-box probes saw slow failure rather than connection refusal, and why `git push` reported 504 before it reported nothing.\n* `rebar/host` `root_disk_used_percent` climbing 87 -> 94% and then STOPPING mid-series, with `GerritReachable` stopping at the same timestamp -> root disk full, publisher dead.\n\nRecovery, chosen because it needs no shell:\n\n1. `create-snapshot` -> `snap-06f35ff3a20fe4a73` (root `vol-0270fcf13709cf472`), for reversibility.\n2. `modify-volume` 30 -> 60 GiB; reached `optimizing` in ~16s, which is already usable.\n3. `reboot-instances`. AL2023 cloud-init runs `growpart` at boot, so the partition AND filesystem expanded with no shell.\n4. Back at **07:10 PDT**: HTTP 200, SSM `Online`, `/dev/nvme0n1p1 60G 29G 32G 48% /`.\n\nThe Elastic IP (`eipalloc-0bfd9c30897fc366a`) meant a stop/start would also have been address-safe, but reboot was sufficient and less disruptive.\n\n**This is an unwedge and must not be recorded as the fix.** The 29 GiB working set is untouched; only headroom changed, 97%+ -> 48%. Whatever produced 29 GiB on a 30 GiB disk will produce 60 GiB on a 60 GiB disk.\n\nAC3 is still open because I could not capture the breakdown: every `du -xh /` and `docker system df` invocation I sent hung in `InProgress` on the freshly rebooted, IO-loaded box, while trivial commands (`df`, `lsblk`) returned immediately. That measurement should be taken while the host is idle. `lsblk` did confirm the 50-GiB data volume `nvme1n1` carries the `gerrit_git`, `gerrit_index`, `gerrit_logs`, `gerrit_plugins` and `gerrit_etc` Docker volumes, so the 29 GiB on ROOT is not Gerrit's repo data — it is Docker images/layers/build cache, which is consistent with every prior incident in the chain.\n\nOne consequence worth noting for the gate-economics record: the outage cost a real false signal. Yesterday's `preodorous-temporary-piglet` records change 2481 burning its retry budget into a **spurious `LLM-Review -1`** from ENOSPC. Disk pressure on this host does not merely slow reviews, it manufactures incorrect review verdicts."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788360136237019001,"uuid":"06cdada7-db63-44ee-a5c4-bcb72dab5441"}